<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>THiddenField</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.THiddenField" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>THiddenField</tt> represents a hidden field on a Web page. The value of the hidden field can be accessed via its <tt>Value</tt> property.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>THiddenField</tt> raises an <tt>OnValueChanged</tt> event if its value is changed during postback.
</p>

<h2>Reading and writing the value</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>Value</tt> holds a string. The control renders it as the <tt>value</tt> attribute of
an <tt>&lt;input type="hidden"&gt;</tt> element, and reads it back from the posted form.
Client script reaches the same element through <tt>ClientID</tt>, so a page carries a
value from the browser to the server without showing it to the user.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:THiddenField ID="Selection" Value="none" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>Data</tt> is another name for <tt>Value</tt>. It exists so that a hidden field works
as the data renderer of a templated control.
</p>

<h2>What the control does not have</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>THiddenField</tt> extends <tt>TControl</tt> rather than <tt>TWebControl</tt>, because
a hidden input has nothing to present. It therefore carries none of the style properties
of a web control, and three members raise a <tt>TNotSupportedException</tt> when a page
uses them.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Member</th><th>Result</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>focus()</tt></td>
	<td>Raises an exception. A hidden element takes no focus.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>EnableTheming</tt></td>
	<td>Raises an exception on assignment. A theme has nothing to style.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>SkinID</tt></td>
	<td>Raises an exception on assignment, for the same reason.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>Validation</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A hidden field is a validation target. A validator points <tt>ControlToValidate</tt> at
it, and reads <tt>Value</tt>. This validates a value that client script writes, such as a
coordinate or a selection made outside the form.
</p>

